# HAVEN package list for the live chroot (Debian bookworm).
# One package per line. Verified present in Debian bookworm unless noted.
# Packages NOT in Debian (wazuh-agent, AWS CLI v2, OpenTofu, k3s, Burp Suite,
# BloodHound, Metasploit) are installed via config/archives/*.list.chroot
# third-party repos or by hook scripts, never listed here.

# --- Desktop (XFCE default) ---
task-xfce-desktop
lightdm

# --- Infrastructure and cloud automation ---
ansible
podman
python3-boto3

# --- Blue team / defensive ---
# suricata is NOT in bookworm; it comes from bookworm-backports (7.0.10),
# enabled by --backports true in the lb config. zeek is not in Debian at
# all (needs the Zeek vendor apt repo, follow-up) and is removed here.
# libhtp2/bookworm-backports: backports suricata needs libhtp2 >=
# 1:0.5.50-1~bpo12+1, but apt's default backports pin (100) makes it pick
# stable's 1:0.5.42-1+deb12u1 and then fail the dependency. The /release
# qualifier forces the backports build; live-build passes list lines
# verbatim to apt-get install (xargs --arg-file, lb_chroot_install-packages),
# so this syntax works. All other suricata deps are satisfied by stable
# versions.
suricata
libhtp2/bookworm-backports
tshark
auditd
clamav
clamav-daemon
openscap-scanner
ssg-debian

# --- Red team / auditing (Debian-packaged subset only) ---
nmap
hydra
nikto
sqlmap
gobuster
hashcat

# --- Firmware (explicit, not auto-discovered) ---
# live-build 3.0~a57 auto-discovers firmware by downloading
# dists/bookworm/Contents-amd64.gz, which Debian does not publish
# (per-component only) -> 404 kills the build. So firmware is named
# explicitly here; firmware-linux pulls firmware-linux-free/nonfree.
firmware-linux
intel-microcode
amd64-microcode

# --- Utilities the layers depend on ---
curl
ca-certificates
git
vim
tmux
htop
net-tools
dnsutils
jq
python3-venv
chrony
nftables
